WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.420
Dynamically allocated objects let a program create storage whose lifetime is controlled by program logic.

2
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:15.080
That is useful for objects returned from factory functions, buffers sized at runtime, data structures that survive a call, and values shared across components.

3
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.620
The lifetime begins only after allocation succeeds.

4
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.980
Allocation is not initialization.

5
00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:27.400
A successful allocation gives storage; initialization gives that storage a known valid state.

6
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:34.060
The code should not pass a partially initialized object to consumers that expect a complete object.

7
00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:40.080
If initialization fails, cleanup should release only the resources already acquired by the constructing code.

8
00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.560
Ownership defines who must release the object.

9
00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:47.500
Caller-owned memory remains the caller's cleanup responsibility.

10
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.000
Callee-owned memory belongs to the callee.

11
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:55.060
Borrowed pointers allow access but do not transfer cleanup authority.

12
00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:59.880
Returned objects and output parameters should document which side owns the result.

13
00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.880
Transfer changes who controls the lifetime.

14
00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:09.620
When a caller passes an object to a container that takes ownership, the caller should stop freeing it after successful insertion.

15
00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:16.920
If a function consumes a pointer, the caller should know whether use of that pointer is allowed after the call and what happens if the function fails.

16
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.120
Freeing an owned dynamic object ends its lifetime for all aliases.

17
00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:26.740
Setting one pointer to NULL may help local cleanup, but it does not update other copies of the pointer.

18
00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:35.000
Structures, caches, callbacks, or borrowed references that still point at the old object must be governed by the same lifetime end.

19
00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:44.960
Defensive dynamic lifetime management keeps allocation, initialization, ownership, transfer, cleanup, and invalidation in one reviewable story.

20
00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:51.540
A pointer is safe only while that story says the object is alive, valid, and usable for the intended operation.
